export default {"id":"drums-level-12","number":12,"title":"Capstone Production","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the drums textbook","summary":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes This is the definitive capstone project of the Drums & Rhythm Programming curriculum. In this module, the producer commits to a single genre lane (Dubstep at 140.00 BPM or Drum & Bass at 174.00 BPM), per","modules":[{"id":"drums-m12-1","code":"12.1","title":"Capstone Production — Finished Drum Track (Dubstep or DnB)","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes This is the definitive capstone project of the Drums & Rhythm Programming curriculum. In this module, the producer commits to a single genre lane (Dubstep at 140.00 BPM or Drum & Bass at 174.00 BPM), per","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-12-1","title":"Section 12.1 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"This is the definitive capstone project of the <strong>Drums &amp; Rhythm Programming</strong> curriculum. In this module, the producer commits to a single genre lane (<strong>Dubstep at 140.00 BPM</strong> or <strong>Drum &amp; Bass at 174.00 BPM</strong>), performs and step-sequences all rhythmic elements using the <strong>Novation Launchkey MK4</strong>, applies the <strong>Master Drum Bus Chain</strong> using stock Ableton Live 12 devices, and renders an unmastered, broadcast-ready 24-bit WAV audio master."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Execute</strong> an end-to-end electronic drum production from initial sound design to final stem export.","<strong>Track</strong> all primary and secondary drum layers using Launchkey MK4 FSR pads and step-sequencing hardware.","<strong>Structure</strong> a 64-bar arrangement adhering strictly to 16-bar binary dancefloor phrasing.","<strong>Mix</strong> a balanced, mono-compatible drum bus peaking at -6.0 dBFS headroom.","<strong>Export</strong> lossless 24-bit / 44.1 kHz WAV master drum files and discrete audio stems.","<strong>Pass</strong> Final Gate Assessment H."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-2","title":"12.1.1: Committing to a Primary Genre Lane","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.1: Committing to a Primary Genre Lane","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A finished track requires absolute commitment to a single production lane:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                     CAPSTONE GENRE SPECIFICATION OPTIONS\n==================================================================================================\nLANE OPTION          PROJECT TEMPO   METRIC IDENTITY         CORE SOUND SOURCES\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOption 1: Dubstep    140.00 BPM      Half-Time (Snare on 3)  DS Kick, DS Snare, DS HH, Operator FM\nOption 2: Drum &amp; Bass 174.00 BPM     Linear Backbeat (5 &amp; 13) Sliced Amen/Think Break + Method B Kick\n==================================================================================================</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>Select ONE option and do not alter the project master tempo.</em>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-3","title":"12.1.2: Reference Track Analysis Log","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.2: Reference Track Analysis Log","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Before authoring MIDI, load your primary commercial reference track into Live on an unwarped audio track and complete the <strong>Master Reference Log</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Document reference Kick peak frequency (Hz).","Document reference Snare fundamental frequency (Hz).","Log reference build-up duration and turnaround fill placement."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-4","title":"12.1.3: The 100% Stock Kit Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.3: The 100% Stock Kit Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Construct your <strong>Master Hybrid Drum Rack</strong> exclusively from native devices:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Pad C1 (36):</strong> <strong>DS Kick</strong> (Tuned to track key center).","<strong>Pad C#1 (37):</strong> <strong>Collision</strong> (Tuned woodblock/rim).","<strong>Pad D1 (38):</strong> <strong>DS Snare</strong> (Layered with Simpler acoustic crack).","<strong>Pad D#1 (39):</strong> <strong>DS Clap</strong> (High-pass filtered @ 600 Hz).","<strong>Pad F#1 (42):</strong> <strong>DS HH</strong> (Mode: 909, Decay: 35 ms, Choke Group 1).","<strong>Pad A#1 (46):</strong> <strong>Drum Sampler</strong> (Open Hat sample, Decay: 500 ms, Choke Group 1).","<strong>Pad G#1 (44):</strong> <strong>Operator</strong> (2-Operator FM Laser Percussion)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-5","title":"12.1.4: Tracking and Sequencing from Launchkey MK4","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.4: Tracking and Sequencing from Launchkey MK4","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Step 1:</strong> Perform the core kick and snare foundation live in <strong>Drum Mode</strong> (<code>Shift + Pad 3</code>).","<strong>Step 2:</strong> Engage <strong>MIDI Overdub (<code>+</code>)</strong> and perform continuous closed hi-hats using <strong>Two-Finger Alternation</strong>.","<strong>Step 3:</strong> Switch to <strong>Sequencer Mode</strong> (<code>Shift + Pad 2</code>) and sequence 16th-note ghost snare rolls and percussion fills directly from the hardware pads."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-6","title":"12.1.5: 64-Bar Structural Arrangement Form","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.5: 64-Bar Structural Arrangement Form","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                    CAPSTONE 64-BAR TIMELINE STRUCTURE\n==================================================================================================\nBARS 01 – 16:   INTRO (Sparse high-frequency tops, high-passed @ 500 Hz, dark room reverb)\nBARS 17 – 32:   BUILD-UP (Snare acceleration 8ths -&gt; 16ths -&gt; 32nds; Bar 32 MUTE GAP)\nBARS 33 – 48:   DROP A (Full primary kick/snare weight; maximum sub-bass cavity space)\nBARS 49 – 56:   MID BREAKDOWN (Atmospheric auxiliary delay throws; kicks muted)\nBARS 57 – 72:   DROP B (Maximum energy; layered claps, ride cymbals, syncopated ghost kicks)\nBARS 73 – 88:   OUTRO (Subtractive deconstruction; DJ mix-out scaffolding)\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-7","title":"12.1.6: Drum Bus Calibration & Lossless WAV Export","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"1. Final Drum Bus Mix Verification:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Utility:</strong> <code>Bass Mono</code> active @ 120 Hz. Output gain staged to peak at <strong>-6.0 dBFS</strong>.","<strong>EQ Eight:</strong> High-pass @ 30 Hz (4x 24 dB slope). Subtractive notch at 300 Hz.","<strong>Drum Buss:</strong> Drive: 15%, Crunch: 20%, Transients: +0.15, Dry/Wet: 50%.","<strong>Glue Compressor:</strong> Attack: 30 ms, Release: Auto, Ratio: 4:1, Peak Gain Reduction: 2–3 dB."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"2. Export Configuration in Live 12:","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Navigate to <strong>File → Export Audio/Video</strong> (<code>Ctrl + Shift + R</code>).","<strong>Rendered Track:</strong> <code>Master</code> (or <code>Drum Group</code>).","<strong>File Type:</strong> <code>WAV</code>.","<strong>Sample Rate:</strong> <code>44100 Hz</code> (or <code>48000 Hz</code>).","<strong>Bit Depth:</strong> <code>24 Bit</code>.","<strong>Dither Options:</strong> <code>No Dither</code> (for unmastered mixdown stems).","Click <strong>Export</strong>."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-1-8","title":"12.1.7: Capstone Production Audit Log","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.1.7: Capstone Production Audit Log","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Document your final production metrics:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                     CAPSTONE PRODUCTION AUDIT CERTIFICATE\n==================================================================================================\nTrack Title            : [ Project Capstone Title ]\nSelected Genre Lane    : [ Dubstep 140.00 BPM | Drum &amp; Bass 174.00 BPM ]\nTotal Measure Length   : [ 64 Bars ]\nPeak Dynamic Ceiling   : [ -6.0 dBFS Peak ]\nStock Devices Deployed : [ Drum Rack, Drum Sampler, DS Kick, DS Snare, DS HH, Operator, Collision ]\nHardware Controller    : [ Novation Launchkey MK4 61 (Pads in Drum &amp; Sequencer Modes) ]\nAudio Export File      : [ Master_Drum_Mixdown_24bit.wav ]\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]},{"type":"step","title":"Final Gate Assessment H Checklist (drums-H)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To clear Gate H and graduate from the core curriculum:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] One finished, fully arranged drum track in Dubstep (140) or DnB (174) exists.","[ ] 100% of drum sounds generated via stock Live 12 devices and native Max for Live instruments.","[ ] Launchkey MK4 hardware pads utilized for real-time tracking and step sequencing.","[ ] Full 64-bar timeline with Locators across all 6 structural sections.","[ ] Drum bus peaks at -6.0 dBFS with active mono low-end summing.","[ ] Lossless 24-bit WAV file rendered and exported to disk."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Why is a peak ceiling of -6.0 dBFS maintained on the master drum mixdown export?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> It leaves clean linear dynamic headroom for subsequent bassline layering, vocal mixing, and master bus limiting without digital inter-sample peak clipping.","<strong>What Dither setting should be selected when exporting a 24-bit unmastered mixdown from Ableton Live?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>No Dither</strong> (dithering is applied only during final 16-bit consumer mastering).","<strong>What is the standard sample rate and bit depth for broadcast-ready electronic music audio stems?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> <strong>24-Bit / 44.1 kHz</strong> (or 24-Bit / 48.0 kHz)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"drums-m12-2","code":"12.2","title":"Capstone Production — Cross-Genre Translation & Peer Pedagogy","blurb":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes True rhythmic mastery is demonstrated by the ability to translate musical ideas across disparate genres and articulate engineering concepts with pedagogical clarity. This final curriculum module explores","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"drums-ov-12-2","title":"Section 12.2 overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section Overview & Learning Outcomes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"True rhythmic mastery is demonstrated by the ability to translate musical ideas across disparate genres and articulate engineering concepts with pedagogical clarity. This final curriculum module explores <strong>Cross-Genre Translation</strong>—converting a Dubstep MIDI skeleton into an authentic Drum &amp; Bass track—and synthesizes the core engineering laws mastered throughout this textbook."},{"type":"prose","html":"Upon completion of this module, the producer will be able to:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Translate</strong> a 140 BPM Dubstep half-time MIDI sequence into a 174 BPM Drum &amp; Bass rolling breakbeat.","<strong>Execute</strong> instant subgenre re-skinning by hot-swapping Drum Racks under an identical MIDI arrangement.","<strong>Formulate</strong> a cohesive teaching framework to instruct beginner producers on electronic drum architecture."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-2-2","title":"12.2.1: Translating 140 Half-Time to 174 Drum & Bass","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.2.1: Translating 140 Half-Time to 174 Drum & Bass","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"A single MIDI performance contains transferable rhythmic DNA:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">==================================================================================================\n                 TRANSLATING DUBSTEP (140) TO DRUM &amp; BASS (174)\n==================================================================================================\nSTEP 1: PROJECT CLOCK SHIFT      Change Master Tempo from 140.00 BPM to 174.00 BPM\nSTEP 2: SNARE MULTIPLICATION     Dubstep has Snare on Step 9 ONLY.\n                                 Add a second Snare on Step 5 and move Step 9 to Step 13.\nSTEP 3: KICK DISPLACEMENT        Dubstep Kick is on Step 1.\n                                 Add a secondary syncopated Kick on Step 11.\nSTEP 4: HI-HAT RESCALING         Convert 8th-note Dubstep hats into 16th-note DnB rolling streams.\n==================================================================================================</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-2-3","title":"12.2.2: One MIDI File, Four Subgenre Kits","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.2.2: One MIDI File, Four Subgenre Kits","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"By maintaining standard General MIDI pad assignments across all your Drum Racks (C1–D\\#2), you can audition completely different subgenre aesthetics instantaneously:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">[ Master 64-Bar MIDI Arrangement ]\n                |\n                +---&gt; Drop on Drum Rack A: \"UK_140_Deep_Kit\"     ---&gt; Dark, spacious sub bass\n                +---&gt; Drop on Drum Rack B: \"Neurofunk_174_Kit\"   ---&gt; Razor-tight, distorted precision\n                +---&gt; Drop on Drum Rack C: \"Liquid_Think_Kit\"    ---&gt; Soulful, rolling acoustic warmth\n                +---&gt; Drop on Drum Rack D: \"Riddim_24_Kit\"       ---&gt; Minimalist, aggressive repetition</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"drums-l12-2-4","title":"12.2.3: Pedagogical Synthesis: Teaching a Beginner","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"12.2.3: Pedagogical Synthesis: Teaching a Beginner","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To test your own mastery, you must be capable of explaining these core engineering laws without jargon or colloquialisms:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>The Law of Negative Space:</strong> Drum tracks derive weight from what is omitted, clearing spectral room for sub-bass displacement.","<strong>The Law of Phase Coherence:</strong> Never layer two drums occupying the identical frequency band without explicit high/low-pass filtering.","<strong>The Law of Dynamic Hierarchy:</strong> Rhythms breathe through velocity contrast; ghost notes must remain below velocity 65.","<strong>The Law of the Clock:</strong> Snare placement dictates perceived tempo feel; Step 9 creates half-time, while Steps 5 and 13 create linear drive."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Laboratory 12.2: Cross-Genre Translation Challenge","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["Take your finished Capstone MIDI file from Section 12.1.","Duplicate the project. If you wrote a Dubstep track, execute the 4-step translation to Drum &amp; Bass at <code>174.00 BPM</code>; if you wrote a DnB track, translate it to Dubstep at <code>140.00 BPM</code>.","Load the corresponding genre Drum Rack.","Export the translated 24-bit WAV file and compare both versions."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Knowledge Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>What two MIDI transformations convert a 140 BPM Dubstep kick/snare pattern into a 174 BPM Drum &amp; Bass pattern?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Shifting project tempo to 174 BPM, moving the snare from Step 9 to Steps 5 and 13 (doubling backbeat frequency), and adding a syncopated secondary kick on Step 11.","<strong>Why is maintaining General MIDI pad compatibility (C1–D\\#2) across all custom Drum Racks essential?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> It allows instantaneous auditioning of different Drum Racks under existing MIDI clips without requiring re-mapping of note pitches.","<strong>What is the Law of Dynamic Hierarchy?</strong>   <em>Answer:</em> Rhythmic momentum and groove are established through contrasting velocity tiers: Primary Accents (110–127), Secondary Accents (80–105), and Ghost Hits (30–65)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":16}],"exercises":[]}]};
